Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 00:01:12 GMT
From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: PHIL: The Mechanics of Synchronicity in a Quantum Omniverse

I suspect many empiricists and rationalists have rejected the existence of
alleged 'synchronistic phenomena' out of hand, as it seems that some kind
of magical (or even magickal) causality would be implied. Rather than quest
for an alternative non-supernatural theory, it's far easier to favour the
'classical' view that apparently meaningful acausal connections are mere
coincidence, and a consequence of the selective filtering of neural nets.
(Indeed, a strong theory at present.) Jung's explanations were very incomplete,
touching on some off-the-mark ideas. E.g., the more holistic approach of the
Chinese mind, Leibniz's 'interrelated monads', and a 'relativisation of time
and space in the unconscious'. I'll try to devise an alternative explanation
based on classical causality and objective external reality. Maybe for good
measure we'll throw some light on why subatomic particles may seem like waves. 

The relevance of this topic to extropians is that, as persons who plan to live
extremely long lives, we have a particular interest in the true nature of
reality. Do we live in one or many universes? Is reality simulated? It would
be a real tragedy if we attained the status of demigods, living lives of
nanotechnological luxury in an anarchocapitalist paradise complete with
intergalactic backups of ourselves, only to hear some Cruel Simulator announce
that It is about to pull the plug -- and we've got ten minutes. But just as an
added delight, we're to be put into a 1,000-year torture time loop a minute
before lights out, and will do a million loops. So, on with the alternative
explanation.....

Some people believe that everything is connected to everything else. Others 
think that a mouse can change the world merely by looking at it. Another view
is that men can do this but not mice or cats. Then there is the idea that the
universe keeps splitting and branching off every time it is faced with a
decision. This is not quite so incredible, but still seems highly unlikely --
why should it split, and as often as all that?

I'll take the view that all the possible permutations of reality exist, and
can be considered as parallel universes. Impossible universes do not exist,
such as those where all our present natural laws have been discovered and
confirmed empirically, and then I managed to violate them and travel around
the world in eighty days on a flying carpet. But contained in the set of all
possible universes are subsets of very similar ones, and it is these that are
especially interesting.  	 

Take a given conscious being. One unique feature is the world-line through
spacetime. But there are two other features unique to each individual that
might be termed the 'consciousness stream' and the 'reality-line'. At each
minimum quantum unit of subjective time that would be seen as the 'present',
the individual has a specific visual representation of the surroundings, is
particularly conscious of a very small number of objects / conditions, half
conscious of other objects, feels a certain way etc. This can be represented
by a complex permutation of memes that are fired or illuminated. In subsequent
units of time the meme complex changes, continuing to reflect the objective
reality experienced. This sequence gives us the consciousness stream (CS) for
that individual. 

Now there will be a subset of parallel universes which, although distinct in
some detail, are so similar in other ways that for a while the CS of an
individual in one universe is exactly the same as those of his/her duplicates
in the others. Sometimes the only differences between two universes might be
too small to ever cause a divergence in this individual's CS. Maybe the
temperature of a leaf of a particular tree in Australia is 20.3 `C in one
universe and 20.2 `C in another. The individual's world-line might differ by
about 1 mm whilst walking in a large open space. A sub-atomic particle could be
in a slightly different position at a given time. OTOH, a butterfly might
happen to flap its wings a few times somewhere, causing an eventual divergence
of the particular CS just as the individual listens to the weather forecast.

We may term the whole set of all possible universes 'the omniverse', and the
subset of universes shared by an individual CS 'a multiverse'. (To say that
the number of universes comprising a multiverse is very, very large is
something of an understatement.) There is a constant divergence as observations
are made, as what was formerly a single CS occupying a multiverse splits up and
branches off. (And sometimes separate multiverses will converge to form a
single multiverse. This could happen if the individual forgets some
distinguishing feature that had previously caused a separation of the CS.)

If the individual was about to observe a subatomic particle, then during the
period that the particle was travelling the individual would be described by
a wavefunction. Within his multiverse, there would be some positions where the
particle never reached in any universe, others where the particle had passed 
through it in a few universes, and others where the particle passed through it
in a large number of universes, depending on the probability amplitude.
However, if the individual was to observe a position to see if the particle
passed through it, the wavefunction would collapse.

Let's say I'm about to switch on the radio. At this point there exist various
configurations of myself in separate universes -- duplicates who are about to
hear differing sounds soon after the first vibrations hit their eardrums. But
at this time they are all still 'me'. They share the same CS, and the universes
are only distinguished by things that I am unaware off; hence this subset of
universes is still a multiverse. So I switch on the radio and some versions
of myself hear music, others hear speech, others hear one particular name 
mentioned, and others hear another name. Plotted against time, the divergence
looks something like this:-


sjw_hears_musicxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx.....................................
sjw_hears_musicxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx....................................
sjw_hears_speechxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx.................................
sjw_hears_speechxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx................................
sjw_hears_the_name_'Bob_Harris'xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
sjw_hears_another_namexxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx..............................

         where x denotes that the CS of the individual sjw was there 
                  in that universe/multiverse at that time.


This brings us to what I term the 'reality-line'. If we think of these parallel
universes as being side by side, as in the above illustration, the reality-line
describes how the CS has diverged. It shows the course taken across the
possible permutations of reality, as opposed to the world-line through
spacetime.

But there is nothing mystical about all this 'diverging of consciousness' or
'sharing of consciousness'. The diverging of an individual's CS is just a
natural consequence of objective external reality. Whenever parts of the
multiverse suddenly become distinct in a way that is known to the individual,
this naturally causes a separation of what was the same CS. When several
consciousness streams are the same, because exactly the same complex 
combination of memes are being fired in the same sequence, the individuals
share consciousness and are effectively the same individual. Now here it may
be asked: however can individuals in parallel universes 'share consciousness'?
Even if they are duplicates in similar universes, they are not even in the
same universe! Doesn't this imply some form of magical telepathic connection? 

It's easier for us to visualise the reality-line and the way divergence occurs
by thinking of all these possible permutations of reality, or parallel
universes, as being somehow side by side. Like a lot of parallel lines. But
this would require a 'superspace', to define that universe A is adjacent to B,
B is adjacent to C etc. Strictly speaking, each of these very-similar parallel
universes are superposed throughout their spacetime, and objects common to each
follow the same world-line (minor discrepancies allowable.) But objects such as
rocks have no awareness of anything whatsoever, particularly alternative
universes. Their motion is described by the laws of classical mechanics or
relativity. Each universe is self-contained -- every event has its cause in the
same universe. A rock travelling in universe A could not collide with a rock
that happened to be in universe B but not A, for instance. 

However, consider a conscious individual who occupies a multiverse that covers
universes A, B and C but not D. The individual has his own unique CS which
incorporates a map of everything known (by him) about the multiverse. His CS
in A is exactly the same as that in the B and C universes. And his world-lines
are the same, or so similar that the difference goes unnoticed. There is a
superposition of A, B and C (and all probabilistic configurations in the
multiverse). Unknown to A`B`C`, we're about to tell him to toss a coin and give
us the result. And we have designated A as that permutation of reality where
the coin came up heads, B is for tails, and C is for that where the individual
refuses to take part in the experiment. So we know that he's about to split
into A, B and C, if the theory is correct. 
 
But in the multiverse ABC we have a situation where the individual A`B`C` is
totally unaware of any difference between A, B and C. He knows that D is an
incorrect description of his world. But he is just an ordinary guy, not really
interested in any of the great mysteries of consciousness, life, the universe
and everything. And unlike a rock, A`B`C` does have a very complex
consciousness stream providing him with a wealth of information about his
environment -- as having all this information was more conducive to survival
than not having it. Since the CS throughout ABC is exactly the same and follows
the same world-line for all practical purposes, 'we' are the only ones who know
that A, B and C are different parallel universes. The only factors that set them
apart are those that A`B`C` will either never know, or does not yet know. As far
as he is concerned, they are the same universe, and so each CS must share
consciousness until something in objective reality causes them to converge.

In theory, some may claim that the consciousness streams in A, B, and C ought
not to be able to 'share' consciousness. But they are all exactly the same,
superpositioned along the same world-line, and no-one ever told A`B`C` that
the 'universes are not the same'. In theory, bumblebees are not supposed to be
aerodynamic enough to fly, but fortunately no-one ever told them so.

Now we want to examine the actual mechanics of how a synchronistic event would
occur.

The 'free-market theory of the mind' is not a new idea. The brain contains
some two million modules each comprising up to 10,000 component neurons, and
each module is trying to build up power whilst at the same time using its
inhibitory neurons to depress adjacent modules. So each module is trying to
'fire' or shout out and at the same time silence its competitors. They each
try to dominate, so at any given time one presumably is the 'winner' and
others are runners up. If I look at a black pair of shoes, whatever module
represents the 'shoe' meme is probably the dominant one, with the 'black' meme
a close second, and runners up are 'pile' and 'magazines' to represent the
pile of magazines that the shoes are placed on. 'Pair' probably already has a
close association with 'shoes'.

Of course, some memes may be so simple that they don't take up a whole module,
or others may take several modules. But there is some activity in the neurons
of every module under normal conditions, so it's truly a complex machine. 

Now we return to the point just as I switch the radio on and the first sound
waves hit my eardrums. Or maybe I've just made some observation, and the
first photons reflected off the object have just struck my retina. A split is
about to occur -- but according to synchronicity, like themes attract each
other and unlike themes repel. If an event had just occured involving the
proper noun 'Bob Harris' you would expect an 'acausally connected' event
involving the same meme to be more likely to occur than would be expected
by pure chance, if synchronicity has any validity.  

How does this occur? Any concept or idea that has recently been contemplated is
far more likely to spring to mind than some long-forgotten item. And whether
a half-forgotten event springs to mind depends on a complex set of 
interconnections that have been laid down in memory. We could say that a
recently fired meme is far more likely to fire again in the near future than a
'cold' meme that is deep in longterm memory, with fading associations. Now 
consider a particular multiverse, where "Bob Harris" is the sound that actually
occured in objective reality. In this case the meme for 'BH' readily fires, and
sure enough, I have a CS which recorded this as the event. I am in a 'BH'
multiverse. 

Next we consider that subset of universes where the sound was "Not 'alf". Or
even "Greetings, pop-pickers". The brain processes the signals from the auditory
nerves. And the battle of the modules begins. In some of these universes the
correct meme wins. But there is a bias towards the 'BH' meme. In other universes
contained within this multiverse, the 'BH' meme, with its unfair advantage,
manages to fire first and silences the correct meme. And this causes the CS to
diverge from the original multiverse and converge with the multiverse where "Bob
Harris" really was the actual sound. As far as any individual is concerned, it
is just our CS, the sequence of meme combinations which are fired, that
determines which multiverse we are in. There is a shift from non-'BH' universes
over to 'BH' universes. And there may be a shift in the reverse direction, but
it is far smaller.

Or let's say you have experienced two events involving a M. de Fortgibu and a
plum-pudding (one in Orleans, and one in Paris). You are then invited to a
gathering where a plum-pudding is on the menu, and remark that the only thing 
missing is M. de Fortgibu. The door opens and someone walks in -- it is a
complete stranger. But in some of these universes the 'de F.' meme fires
because it is in such a state of readiness. And your CS has shifted over to
a multiverse where the gatecrasher really was M. de Fortgibu, and just
happened to lose his way and stumble on the party by accident. 

The result of these shifts would mean that the probability of an individual
experiencing a synchronistic event should be higher than its chance
probability. All of the experiences have causes in objective reality. And the
course of the reality-line -- the way the CS has diverged and converged  --
exists theoretically in objective reality, taking into account all universes
involved. But subjectively an individual would tend to experience synchronistic
events, and would also find that others tended to as well. Each individual
would have his/her own unique reality-line. No two individuals would ever
diverge in the same way. 

So if synchronistic events were found to have a higher occurrence than their
chance probability, this would support the many-worlds view. Some universes 
would be simulated, but at least we would have plenty of time to attempt to
devise a strategy for avoiding them. And this would be preferable to being
stuck in a single universe which turned out to be a simulation. 

